<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cafer Seydahmet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a>–<a href="/years/1960">1960</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Cafer Seydahmet Kırımer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cafer Seydahmet was a Crimean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> politician and writer. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a> he served with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noman-celebicihan">Noman Çelebicihan</a> in the directorate of the Crimean People's Republic of 1917 to 1918, responsible for foreign and military affairs. When the Bolsheviks overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> he went into exile, spending most of his later life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> as a champion of the Crimean Tatar cause.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cafer-seydahmet.jpg" alt="Cafer Seydahmet" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cafer_Seydamet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
